
  The rule that makes this worth the structure: [1m[38;5;153mmodel/[39m is pure and knows nothing about storage or the DOM.[0m A [38;5;153mTask[39m is
  data, and the functions over it are total and side-effect-free. That's what lets sub-project 2 write reminder rules as
  plain predicates — [38;5;153misOverdue(task, now)[39m — and unit-test them with zero setup and no fake clock plumbing beyond
  passing [38;5;153mnow[39m in.

  [38;5;153mstore[39m is the single source of truth and the only thing that mutates. It exposes [38;5;153mgetState()[39m, a set of intent-named
  commands ([38;5;153maddTask[39m, [38;5;153mtoggleTask[39m, …), and [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m. Persistence hangs off a subscription rather than being called
  from the UI, so no call site can forget to save. [38;5;153mrender[39m is a pure function of state — the whole list redraws on
  change, which at personal-task-list scale is correct and about two orders of magnitude cheaper than the bugs that
  diffing by hand would cost.

  Why this shape specifically: [38;5;153msubscribe()[39m is the seam sub-project 2 plugs into. The reminder engine becomes another
  subscriber that watches for state changes and emits notifications — no rework of the task core when we get there.

